{"anu": [{"id": "anu", "headword": "अनु", "headword_slp1": "anu", "fold": "anu", "headword_language": "sa", "source": "vedic-index-macdonell-keith", "senses": [{"gloss": "Anu, Ānava.--Grassmann and Roth see in these words designations of people foreign to the Aryans. But it is clear that they denote a special people, the Anus, who are mentioned with the Yadus, Turvaśas, Druhyus, and Pūrus, with the Druhyus, and with the Turvaśas, Yadus, and Druhyus. It is also a fair conclusion from their mention in a passage of the Rigveda that they dwelt on the paruṣṇī. The inference that the Bhṛgus were connected with this tribe is much more doubtful, for it rests solely on the fact that in one place the Bhṛgus and Druhyus are mentioned together, and not Anus and Druhyus. ānava is used as an epithet of agni, but also in the sense of ‘Anu prince,’ in one case in conjunction with turvaśa.\nFoot notes St. Petersburg Dictionary.\nZimmer, Altindisches Leben, 125.\nRv. i. 108, 8.\nRv. vii. 18, 14.\nviii. 10, 5.\nRv. viii. 74. 15, compared with vii. 18, 14.\nOldenberg, Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft, 42, 221, n. 1.\nRv. vii. 18, 4.\nRv. viii. 74, 4. Cf. Rv. v. 31, 4, where the Anus prepare the chariot of the Aśvins.\nRv. vii. 18, 13.\nviii. 4, 1. Apparently as prince also in vi. 62, 9. Cf. kuruṅga.\nCf. Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 205; Hillebrandt, Vedische Mythologie, 3, 153; Macdonell, Sanskrit Literature, 154.", "gloss_language": "en"}], "synonyms": ["aanava", "anu", "ānava", "आनव", "అను", "ఆనవ", "ಅನು", "ಆನವ"], "synonyms_slp1": ["aanava", "anu", "ānava", "Anava", "అను", "ఆనవ", "ಅನು", "ಆನವ"]}], "anu-ksatf": [{"id": "anu-kzattf", "headword": "अनु-क्षत्तृ", "headword_slp1": "anu-kzattf", "fold": "anu-ksatf", "headword_language": "sa", "source": "vedic-index-macdonell-keith", "senses": [{"gloss": "Anu-kṣattṛ.--This word occurs in the list of victims at the Puruṣamedha, and means, according to Mahīdhara, ‘an attendant on the door-keeper,’ and, according to Sāyaṇa, ‘an attendant on the charioteer’ (sārathi). See also kṣattṛ.\n3) On taittirīya brāhmaṇa, loc. cit.\nFoot notes Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 11; 73; taittirīya brāhmaṇa, iii. 4, 9, 1.\nOn Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, loc. cit.", "gloss_language": "en"}], "synonyms": ["anu", "anu-kShattR", "anu-kShattRRi", "anu-kShattRi", "anu-kShattRu", "anu-kṣattṛ", "anu-xattR", "anukShattR", "anukShattRRi", "anukShattRi", "anukShattRu", "anukṣattṛ", "anuxattR", "kShattR", "kShattRRi", "kShattRi", "kShattRu", "kṣattṛ", "xattR", "अनु", "अनुक्षत्तृ", "क्षत्तृ", "అను", "అను-క్షత్తృ", "అనుక్షత్తృ", "క్షత్తృ", "ಅನು", "ಅನು-ಕ್ಷತ್ತೃ", "ಅನುಕ್ಷತ್ತೃ", "ಕ್ಷತ್ತೃ"], "synonyms_slp1": ["anu", "anu-kShattR", "anu-kShattRRi", "anu-kShattRi", "anu-kShattRu", "anu-kṣattṛ", "anu-xattR", "anukShattR", "anukShattRRi", "anukShattRi", "anukShattRu", "anukṣattṛ", "anuxattR", "kShattR", "kShattRRi", "kShattRi", "kShattRu", "kṣattṛ", "xattR", "anu", "anukzattf", "kzattf", "అను", "అను-క్షత్తృ", "అనుక్షత్తృ", "క్షత్తృ", "ಅನು", "ಅನು-ಕ್ಷತ್ತೃ", "ಅನುಕ್ಷತ್ತೃ", "ಕ್ಷತ್ತೃ"]}], "anu-cara": [{"id": "anu-cara", "headword": "अनु-चर", "headword_slp1": "anu-cara", "fold": "anu-cara", "headword_language": "sa", "source": "vedic-index-macdonell-keith", "senses": [{"gloss": "Anu-cara.--This is a general expression for an ‘attendant’ (the feminine being Anucarī), but it is not often used.\nFoot notes Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 13; taittirīya brāhmaṇa, iii. 4, 7, 1.\nSatapatha brāhmaṇa, xiii. 5, 4, 27 (apparently slave girls are meant, 400 being enumerated).", "gloss_language": "en"}], "synonyms": ["anu", "anu-cara", "anu-chara", "anucara", "anuchara", "cara", "chara", "अनु", "अनुचर", "चर", "అను", "అను-చర", "అనుచర", "చర", "ಅನು", "ಅನು-ಚರ", "ಅನುಚರ", "ಚರ"], "synonyms_slp1": ["anu", "anu-cara", "anu-chara", "anucara", "anuchara", "cara", "chara", "anu", "anucara", "cara", "అను", "అను-చర", "అనుచర", "చర", "ಅನು", "ಅನು-ಚರ", "ಅನುಚರ", "ಚರ"]}], "anu-mati": [{"id": "anu-mati", "headword": "अनु-मति", "headword_slp1": "anu-mati", "fold": "anu-mati